                <pb facs="0841" n="743" sameAs="frus:frus1920v01/pg743"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.119/320: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Tenney</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-05-17T17:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">May 17, 1920—5
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received May 17—2:35 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>109. Referring to the Department’s April 23, noon and May 16 [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">14</hi>], 1 p.m. No confirmation of report of Belgian
                    shipment of arms. The dealing in aeroplanes has been reported already. The
                    Italian Minister assures me emphatically that no Italian munitions have been
                    delivered nor can I obtain proof his statement is inaccurate. Steel and copper
                    have been imported by merchants of different nationalities, including American,
                    nominally for mints or other enterprises which have ultimately reached the
                    arsenals. It is generally believed that the Japanese have been dealing in
                    munitions but proof is not obtainable.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Tenney</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
